[Line "Bayonet 9.b4 Nh5 10.Re1: 12.Bf3 c6"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: the Bayonet, 9.b4"]
[Summary "White's knight comes to e6, Black takes it and a white pawn lands there. Black breaks the centre open with ...c6 and ...d5 and reaches a level middlegame."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 { [%cal Rc4c5,Rf3g5]
The Bayonet. White prepares c5 with one move and keeps the knight on f3, so that ...f5 can be answered by
Ng5 and Ne6. The pawn on b4 is loose, and f4 is weak now that the c1-bishop has other duties. }
9... Nh5 { [%cal Gh5f4,Gf7f5] [%csl Yf4] In the 9.Ne1 lines the knight goes to d7 to hold up c5. Here c5
cannot be held up: 9...Nd7 10.c5 simply gives White his break with tempo. So the knight goes where it
has a target, f4, and the f-pawn is released at the same time. }
( 9... a5 { The main alternative, and a sound one for those who want to avoid the forcing lines with Ng5.
Black breaks up the queenside before c5 arrives. After 10.bxa5 he recaptures with the rook or plays
10...c5; after the more testing 10.Ba3 he gets the c5-square and an active bishop. } 10. Ba3 axb4 11. Bxb4
Nd7 12. a4 Bh6 { Black follows with ...f5 and ...Nc5 or ...Nf6. White has the b-file and the a-pawn; the
game is slower and more positional than after 9...Nh5. } )
10. Re1 { [%cal Re2f1] The main line. The rook makes room so that ...Nf4 can be met by Bf1, keeping the
bishop, and it will stand well on the e-file once ...f5 has been played. } 10... f5 { Black sticks to his
plan. 10...Nf4 11.Bf1 a5 is a system of its own, with play on the queenside. } 11. Ng5 { [%cal Rg5e6,Re2h5]
The knight heads for e6 and uncovers the bishop's attack on h5. } 11... Nf6 { The knight has to move, and
on f6 it attacks e4 again and takes g4 away from White's pieces. 11...Nf4 12.Bxf4 exf4 is the other
line: Black gets the long diagonal, but e6 is still available to the knight and theory prefers White. } 12. Bf3 { The bishop defends e4 and
aims down the long diagonal at b7, which will matter once the centre opens. } 12... c6 { [%cal Gh7h6,Gd6d5]
[%csl Ye6,Rd5] The key move of the whole variation. Black attacks d5, the pawn that would support a
knight or a pawn on e6. After ...h6, Ne6 Bxe6, dxe6 the pawn on e6 is cut off from its base and Black's
centre is free to advance with ...d5. Playing ...h6 first comes to the same thing. }
13. Be3 { Development, with c5 or b5 to follow. }
( 13. Bb2 { The bishop looks at e5, and there is a concrete idea behind it. } 13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6
fxe4 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Rxe4 d5 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Rxe5 { The point: an exchange sacrifice for the long
diagonal and the pawn on e6. } 19... Bxe5 20. Bxe5 Qb6 { The queen attacks b4 and e6 and leaves the pin.
White has a pawn and the dark squares for the exchange; Black's king is safer than it looks and the
e6-pawn can still be rounded up. Theory regards the chances as level. } )
( 13. b5 { White tries to keep d5 supported by opening the c-file himself. } 13... cxd5 14. cxd5 h6 15. Ne6
Bxe6 16. dxe6 fxe4 17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. Bxe4 d5 19. Ba3 { White lets the bishop on e4 go and takes the pinned knight instead. } 19... dxe4 20. Qxd8
Rfxd8 21. Bxe7 Re8 { The e6-pawn falls and the ending is equal. } )
13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 { Now, with d5 gone, opening the centre is right: Black exchanges the
pieces that could blockade his pawns. } 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 d5 { [%csl Re6] The centre advances with
tempo and the e6-pawn is isolated deep in Black's camp. } 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Bc2 b6 { [%cal Gd8d6,Gf8f6]
Takes c5 from the bishop, which would otherwise pin the knight against the rook, and prepares ...Qd6. }
20. Qg4 { [%cal Gf8f6,Gd8d6] The queen defends e6 and eyes g6.

**Verdict:** equal, and well explored. Black has a broad centre and will attack e6 with ...Rf6 and
...Qd6, or advance with ...e4 when the long diagonal is under control. White has the bishops and
pressure on d5 with Bb3 and Rad1. If the e6-pawn falls without compensation Black is better; if the
centre is blockaded on the light squares White is. Most games from here are drawn. } *

[Line "Bayonet 10.Re1: 12.f3 Kh8"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: the Bayonet, 9.b4"]
[Summary "White defends e4 with the pawn and keeps the bishop for the queenside. Black answers with the prophylactic ...Kh8 and wins the e6-pawn by regrouping his knights."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. Re1 f5 11. Ng5
Nf6 12. f3 { [%csl Ye3] The solid choice. The e4-pawn is secure for good and the bishop stays on e2 to
support c5. In return White weakens the dark squares round his king and puts no pressure on b7, so
Black has time. } 12... Kh8 { [%cal Ge7g8,Gf6g8] Prophylaxis with two points. After Ne6 and dxe6 White
often gains time on the a2-g8 diagonal with c5 and Qb3 or Bc4; with the king on h8 nothing comes with
check. And g8 is free for a knight, which is how the e6-pawn will be surrounded. }
13. Rb1 { A useful waiting move: the rook protects b4 and leaves the long diagonal. Against White's other
tries (13.Be3, 13.b5, 13.c5) the ideas are the same: ...h6 or ...Ne8 to question the knight, then the e6-pawn. }
( 13. Ne6 { The knight does not wait to be asked. } 13... Bxe6 14. dxe6 Nh5 { [%cal Gh5f4,Gg7f6] Black has
a clear route to the pawn: the bishop goes to f6 and the knight to g7. The knight also eyes f4. } 15. g3
Bf6 { [%cal Gh5g7,Gg7e6] Next ...Ng7 and ...Nxe6. White has to find counterplay with c5 before the pawn
goes; if he does not, Black is simply a pawn up with the better structure. } )
13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. fxe4 Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4] The knight is finally developed to the
square it was driven from on move eight, and it is on its way to d4. } 17. Nd5 Ng8 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gd8e8,Gc7c6]
The second knight steps back to let the queen and rook out and to cover e7.

**Verdict:** the e6-pawn is surrounded. Black will play ...Nd4, evict the d5-knight with ...c6 and
collect the pawn with a knight or with the queen from e8. White keeps the bishop pair and some
initiative while this takes place, which is enough to hold the balance and no more. } *

[Line "Bayonet 10.g3"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: the Bayonet, 9.b4"]
[Summary "White takes f4 from the knight at the cost of a target on g3. Black plays ...f5 and ...f4 and the fight is again about the knight on g5 and the pawn on e6."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. g3 { [%csl Rg3,Rh3]
White keeps the knight out of f4 for good. The price is paid on the light squares around the king,
and the g3-pawn gives Black's f-pawn something to bite on. } 10... f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3
( 12. Bf3 { As in the 10.Re1 line, and Black uses the same recipe. } 12... c6 { [%cal Gh7h6] After ...h6
and Ne6 Bxe6, dxe6 the pawn on e6 is cut off, and the extra move g3 has only weakened White's king. The
bishop can drop back to g2, which is solid and gives Black time for ...Qc8 or ...fxe4 and ...d5. } )
12... f4 { [%cal Gf4g3,Gf6h5] Now that g3 is there to be attacked the pawn goes forward. Black threatens
to open the king with ...fxg3 and ...Nh5, and the knight on g5 is short of squares: ...h6 will force it
to e6. } 13. Kg2 { The king defends g3 and h3. 13.gxf4 exf4 would open the long diagonal and give Black
h5 and e5 for his knights. }
( 13. b5 { White ignores the kingside, and the result is one of the best-known forcing lines of the
Bayonet. } 13... fxg3 14. hxg3 h6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Qc8 { [%cal Gc8e6,Gc8h3] The queen attacks e6 and
h3 at once. } 17. Nd5 Qxe6 18. Nxc7 Qh3 { Black gives the rook: the threat is ...Qxg3+. } 19. Rf2 Nxe4
{ Taking the knight exposes the king to ...Rxf2 and the queen checks. With accurate play from both sides
the complications burn out to equality, and White is the one who has to know them. } )
13... c6 { [%cal Gh7h6,Gd8c8] The same idea as in the 10.Re1 lines: d5 is undermined before the knight is
driven to e6.

**Verdict:** after ...h6, Ne6 Bxe6, dxe6 Black picks up the e6-pawn with ...Qc8, and White's
compensation is the bishop pair and pressure on d6 with Qb3, Rd1 and c5. The pawn on f4 keeps White's
king permanently uneasy. Theory rates the position as balanced; in practice Black's play is easier. } *

[Line "Bayonet: 10.c5 and 10.Nd2"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: the Bayonet, 9.b4"]
[Summary "White allows ...Nf4. Black gets the bishop pair and the long diagonal after 10.c5, and a kingside attack with tempo after 10.Nd2."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. c5 { White
breaks at once and does not mind the knight coming to f4. }
( 10. Nd2 { The knight is going to c4 after c5, to attack d6. As Kotronias points out, the drawback is
obvious: Black's knight reaches f4 with gain of time. } 10... Nf4 11. a4 { White gives the bishop a
square on a3 and the rook one on a3 or a2 before deciding about the bishop on e2. }
( 11. Bf3 { Keeping the bishop. } 11... f5 { The standard attacking move; ...g5 and ...g4 will come with
tempo against the bishop. } )
11... f5 12. Bf3 g5 { [%cal Gg5g4] Black gives up the e4-square to get the e7-knight into the game. }
13. exf5 Nxf5 14. g3 { White has to drive the knight away. } 14... Nh3+ 15. Kg2 Qd7 { [%cal Gf5d4,Gg5g4]
Kotronias's main line. The queen lines up behind the f5-knight, so that ...Nd4 will uncover it against
h3, and ...g4 is coming to support the knight. The careless
16.Nb3 Nd4 17.Be4 loses at once to 17...Nxf2, as in Petrosian-Rashkovsky, 1974. Black has full play for
his slightly worse structure. } )
10... Nf4 11. Bxf4 { Otherwise the bishop on e2 goes, or the knight stays on f4 for ever. } 11... exf4
{ [%cal Gg7c3,Gg6g5] [%csl Ye5] The character of the game has changed. Black has the bishop pair, an open
long diagonal and e5 for his pieces; White has a lead in development, the d4-square and pressure on the
c-file. The f4-pawn can become weak if Black is careless. } 12. Rc1 { The rook takes the knight out of
the bishop's reach and prepares cxd6 and Nb5. } 12... h6 { [%cal Gg6g5,Ge7g6] Black supports the f4-pawn
with ...g5 and gives the knight g6, from where it reaches e5. The move also rules out Ng5-e6.

**Verdict:** a balanced fight between structure and bishops. Black keeps the queenside closed as long as
he can (...a6 against Nb5, ...dxc5 only when it wins time), builds up with ...g5, ...Ng6 and ...Ne5,
and eventually pushes ...g4. White plays for cxd6 and an invasion on c7 or for e5 to free his pieces. } *
